I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern format, and the frozen analysis tooling. Then I’ll build the simplest chemically plausible multiphase model, refine it in GSAS-II, and verify that every coherent reflection family is accounted for before writing the deliverables.

The chemical setting strongly suggests a low-temperature partial reaction—likely some combination of BaWO₄ and residual/decomposed precursors—but I’m treating that only as a starting hypothesis. I’m now querying the frozen COD index and extracting observed peak families so the phase inventory is driven by the scan.

The peak mapping is now internally consistent for two ordinary phases: orthorhombic witherite BaCO₃ accounts for the 24.21/24.65°, 34–35°, 42–47°, and 55–56° families, while monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the interleaved 23.43/23.91° family and its higher-angle counterparts. I’m moving this into quantitative whole-pattern refinements and will use any coherent residuals—not a marginal Rwp change—to decide whether BaWO₄ or another phase is actually present.

The first coordinate branch exposed an important issue: an unconstrained intensity improvement can push oxygen positions to their bounds. I’ve kept the pre-coordinate checkpoint and am screening the trial geometry now; it will be rejected unless carbonate groups and WO₆ octahedra remain chemically credible.

The geometry screen rejects the old 1960 WO₃ coordinates as a final model: one crystallographic W site is effectively five-coordinate (the sixth W–O contact is ~2.87 Å). I’m therefore testing the better-resolved room-temperature monoclinic references of the same composition; this follows the physical-model criterion even if the older model can be forced to a slightly lower residual.

The residual intensity trend is diagnostic rather than a new reflection family: the strongest low-angle peaks are overcalculated while the same phases’ 42–56° reflections are undercalculated. That is the signature expected from Bragg–Brentano surface roughness, so I’m testing that specimen term next before considering texture or another phase.

The two-phase structural model is complete and physical: witherite BaCO₃ plus monoclinic WO₃, with no independently supported BaWO₄ family. I’m now testing the last justified profile refinement—directional broadening of the WO₃ peaks—before freezing and exporting the exact replayable project.

The width residuals support a modest [010]-type anisotropy in WO₃ and a stronger but still physical [010] anisotropy in BaCO₃; the other trial axes fit worse. I’m converging that selected branch now, then I’ll clean stale refinement controls so the submitted project replays without hidden boundary artifacts.

The selected model has converged on the complete 8,301-point grid at Rwp 5.94%, with stable fractions near 53.24 wt% BaCO₃ and 46.76 wt% WO₃. I’m exporting directly from that cleaned checkpoint and will then independently reload the deliverables to verify project/CIF/fraction/profile consistency.

All requested artifacts are now written. The exact submitted project has passed an independent no-refinement GSAS-II replay: the original grid and observations matched, both phase fractions matched exactly, both CIFs matched the project exports, and the replay reported no physical-profile warnings. I’m doing the final schema and file-integrity check now.

Completed. The refined crystalline composition is:

- BaCO₃, witherite: **53.24 wt%**
- Monoclinic WO₃: **46.76 wt%**

Full-grid fit: **Rwp 5.9389%**, **GOF 1.7096**. BaWO₄ was not reliably detected; a trace amount around 1 wt% or less cannot be excluded.

